{
	title: 'Browser-native web transcript scrolling',
	spec: 'The transcript uses normal document flow and remembers whether the reader is following the live end. Sending or tapping the return button follows live; leaving the end yields to the reader. While reading, appends and streaming below never write the scroll position. Keep the visible content in place when earlier history is inserted. Return to a tab at its remembered visible card and offset, or at the end for live and new tabs. Preserve on-demand history paging, links, selection, composer resizing and the native scrolling gesture. Keep the browser regression checks for these behaviors.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Chrome native anchoring in column-reverse does not keep the place when a big table reflows inside the card being read (removed reflow.ts did). Accepted for simplicity; the reading-reflow browser check was dropped.',
		"Bug fix: Safari 26 (iPhone, Hal.app WKWebView) lacks overflow-anchor, so the browser-only design lost the bottom and the reading place there. Invariant: a bottom reader stays at the bottom and a reader above keeps the same row in sight in every engine. Saving the place on Hal's own scroll events made it lag behind a fast stream (select-all test drifted 2102 px). Checked on iPhone Safari by the user.",
		'Bug fix: in WKWebView (macOS 27.2) a bottom reader sits at scrollTop -1 as rows grow; the first growth fired a scroll event at -1, which mark() took for a reader above, so the view froze at the first rows of a fast 1000-step turn (ADEMark A11). Invariant: a gap under 2 px is the bottom. Reproduced and checked with a WKWebView harness against the ADEMark replay server.',
		'Streaming cost grew with row count: cursorKey read rows() and jobs/editable were fresh Sets each event, so every card binding reran per delta (290 ms main thread per new row at ~1000 rows, headless Chrome long-2000 replay). Invariant: a card reruns only when its own cursor/last/job/edit value changes; per-row memos and set-equal memos in Transcript.tsx (~40 ms per row after).',
		'Streaming still cost O(rows): Solid 2 marks every node downstream of a written signal (markNode is transitive, equality only stops reruns), and each card read memos of props.view. Invariant: no card binding depends on props.view; cards read a createProjection store keyed by row key (marks bits) and shared leaves, so an event notifies only cards whose marks change. Solid 2 effects rerun whenever their source reruns, equal or not: put a memo before an effect fed by a fresh object (Tabs Marker). Streaming Markdown measures its peak once a frame; new cards are observed via MutationObserver. Headless Chrome long-2000: not drawn at 69 s before, drawn in 22 s after (host ~27 s).',
		'Bug fix: a tab that grew while shown kept its first row pinned, so showing it again built every card (ADEMark A11: 2000 rows, ~10 s in WKWebView, 1.3 s headless Chrome). Invariant: a tab shown again at the bottom builds only its newest rows (Transcript reopened); a reader above keeps its rows. Headless Chrome long-2000 switch: 1.2-1.6 s before, under 60 ms after.',
		'Scrolling up added 60 earlier rows in one frame: a 66 ms frame in headless Chrome, 4-5 dropped frames per scroll in ADEMark A11 (WKWebView). Invariant: rows load 5 at a time near the top; headless Chrome long-2000 scroll: no frame over 17 ms in 3 of 4 tries (one 33 ms).',
		'ADEMark A11 receipts stop moving at step 973 because the 20 wheel events end there, not because rows ran out; preloading (c4150ac9, f3e661e5, reverted) left gaps unchanged (median 4). The benchmark OCRs every captured frame synchronously on its capture queue, a possible cause of its frame gaps.',
		'To keep typing and scrolling responsive, an idle transcript does not expand its rendered history. Browser Find searches rows already rendered.',
		'The web return-to-live button floats at the lower right with a 48px thumb target. It counts new transcript cards since the last visit to live; chunks and prepends do not count. Terminal unchanged.',
		'The web transcript uses normal document flow. Live follows growth; reading history never writes scrollTop for output below the reader. Save a stable visible row and its offset when leaving a tab.',
		'On engines without native anchoring, near-top prepends wait for touch momentum to settle; status-bar taps explicitly seek entry 1 across history pages.',
		'Deep history keeps at most 200 transcript rows mounted. A linked card and the live end must each be reachable without mounting the intervening rows.',
		'Top (status-bar tap) and End share one jump rule (scroll.ts near/dim/land): a target under 2 screens away with no fling running scrolls smoothly; otherwise the transcript fades out (150 ms, ease-in-out; 600 ms when earlier history must load first), jumps with overflow hidden for a frame, then fades back in (reverse of the fade-out). The progress box appears only after 400 ms, with one fixed text, so fast jumps show nothing.',
		'Bug fixed: a Top tap during an iOS fling landed on the first message, then the momentum stale scroll event (scrollTop past the content end) sent the view to the live end. iOS momentum outlives scrollTop writes; only overflow: hidden stops it. Invariant: a jump during a fling lands on its target and stays there.',
		'Transcript effect callbacks read signals only inside untrack: the browser tests fail on Solid STRICT_READ_UNTRACKED diagnostics (task f09).',
		'Bug fix: Transcript window slides and showEnd() mounted up to 200 virtual-scrolled cards without scroll.quiet(), triggering 300 ms opacity Web Animations (cardMotion.enter) on every mounted card during iOS fling-ups and exhausting GPU/IOSurface layer memory (WebKit Jetsam crash). Invariant: virtual-window row shifts and end jumps mount inside scroll.quiet() so revealed history cards never start enter animations. Verified on iPhone Safari by the user.',
		'Bug fix (53-gst): the window of 200 rows stood the unmounted rows in an estimated margin, and settling it wrote scrollTop; on iOS a fling continued from its old offset after the write, so the view jumped to a random place (scroll probe log). Invariant: the web transcript never estimates heights. Rows mounted or removed above the reader are measured and the row in view keeps its exact place (scroll.keep); without overflow-anchor the window slides only while no finger, fling or bounce moves the view, and the compensating write holds overflow hidden for a frame. Only the reader, tab switches, Top/End/links and following the live end move the view. A fling up stops at the first mounted row; the window slides 16 rows at a time after it settles.',
		'Bug fix (53-gst): when the keyboard closed on iPhone, the transcript grew 390 px but iOS kept scrollTop past the new end; follow saw no gap, so blank space stayed under the last line until a touch snapped it back (a jump). Invariant: when the transcript or its rows resize and no fling or bounce runs, a scroll position past the end is set to the end at once (scroll.ts resized).',
		"Bug fix (55-bib): learning anchoring from measured slides (09ac48a1) blocked mid-fling slides on iOS Safari 27.2, so upward flicks hit the oldest mounted row and bounced until motion settled (probe log b870c68f, 14:18). Reverted 09ac48a1 back to CSS.supports('overflow-anchor', 'auto') so upward flicks keep loading older rows via scroll.keep with no top wall."
	],
